  "configuration_id": "config_031",
  "reason": "Evidence: The current best (config_019, acc=0.639) uses pca_n2000 as the RNA encoder, and all completed experiments share this encoder. RNA is the dominant signal (RNA-only multiprofile avgpool: 0.591 vs. image-only: ~0.21–0.23), yet no learned RNA encoder (scvi_n200, scgpt) has been tested. Uncertainty: It is unknown whether a learned latent representation (scvi_n200) captures more discriminative structure than PCA in the multiprofile multimodal setting. Question: Does replacing pca_n2000 with scvi_n200 improve validation accuracy when all other settings (multiprofile, resnet50, avgpool, weighted fusion) are held at their best-performing values? Why this experiment: config_031 is a clean single-variable comparison against config_019 (only the RNA encoder changes), isolating the representation effect in the highest-performing regime. Because RNA drives most of the signal, a change in RNA encoder is likely to have a larger impact than changing the weak image encoder, making this the most informative use of the next budget slot. If scvi_n200 helps, we immediately identify a new best configuration; if not, we rule out a major design axis without having spent budget on more complex multimodal variants."
}

